---
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";
---

<StudyLayout>
  <section>
    <h1>Rock planets v0.1 — question test and reference brief</h1>
    <p>
      12 November 2026.
      <a href="/lab/studies/mercury-2026-11-12">Study opening</a>.
    </p>
    <h2>Current question test · rerun on 12 November 2026</h2>
    <p>
      Independent, clean-context, read-only review of the revised section 01,
      after the mechanics-boundary correction. Prior stamps and findings were
      not used to derive this result.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>One subject — Pass.</strong>
      One reusable object: airless rock planets across sizes and stellar
      surroundings. The complete registry has no other open study owning the
      answer; adjacent mechanics and other kinds are bounded.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Grounded — Pass with findings.</strong>
      The drawing, principles and mechanical context have code/spec support.
      Surface, colour, illumination, detail and boundary treatments can be
      inspected together; comparison conditions remain imprecise.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Consequence — Pass.</strong>
      The shared rock-planet drawing changes inside charging fields across
      modes. Mercury is an application.
    </p>
    <h3>Findings and responses</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Comparison conditions were unspecified: “a production star” does not
        identify its field profile or the physical-size/viewing-scale bounds.
        Response: section 01 now requires each comparison to state its star
        profile, radii and camera scale. Initial exploration will use practice
        G7 and the current roster’s radii 18, 30 and 45 at matched camera
        scales; exact views must be recorded with the examples. These are
        starting fixtures, not family limits or accepted tuning.
      </li>
      <li>
        The opening substantially exceeded its normal 150–250-word editing
        target, carrying scope management, lore and delivery detail. Response:
        it was shortened; the home-system lore remains recorded below rather
        than repeated in full. These editorial corrections were not
        independently tested again.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      No additional finding on the mechanics boundary: section 01 treats
      existing motion, gravity and contact as context, leaves size–mass
      unresolved and bounds mechanical follow-up. Code support does not freeze
      current tuning.
    </p>
    <h3>Evidence and limits</h3>
    <p>
      Checked the complete study registry and study contract;
      packages/core/src/lib/assets/planet.ts (body and outline),
      packages/core/src/lib/physics/Physics.ts (orbit, gravity and contact),
      packages/core/src/lib/entities/Renderer.ts (orbit/influence treatments);
      docs/design.md (procedural art and readability);
      docs/specs/game/world/planets/spec.md (reopened flat-disc presentation),
      gravity-field/spec.md and star/spec.md (mechanics and varying field
      profiles); docs/specs/game/test-flight/spec.md (fixed practice system);
      and NASA’s linked Mercury facts (rock, craters and thin exosphere).
    </p>
    <p>
      Not checked: browser rendering, keyboard/1280px presentation, gameplay
      feel, proposed visuals, reference-gallery quality, feasibility,
      implementation scope or design acceptance. Parts 02–04 remain unfilled;
      the whole-study rubric was not applied. The reviewer ran no tests and
      edited no files.
    </p>
    <h2>Boundary correction after review</h2>
    <p>
      The family-level review verified that existing mechanics had repository
      support; it did not establish a designer decision to freeze their details.
      Section 01 previously treated current gravity, collision, orbit and
      charging rules as fixed. That was too broad. Orbits, contact and
      gravitational pull now supply starting context. Body size does not settle
      mass, and orbit HUD colour is open where it affects the visual result.
      Current numbers can make comparisons reproducible without becoming
      accepted tuning.
    </p>
    <p>
      The subject remains the airless rock planet inside a charging field.
      Mechanical questions arising from an example must be named and bounded
      before experiments change them; they do not require a general mechanics
      redesign within this study. No production constants or contracts were
      changed by this correction. The question wording is unchanged and the
      correction was not independently reviewed again.
    </p>
    <h2>Earlier framing review — superseded</h2>
    <p>
      The Mercury-only opening received One subject — Pass, Grounded — Pass with
      findings, and Consequence — Pass. That review confirmed the current
      innermost body’s placement, distinguished supplied lore from
      implementation, and identified stale claims in the planet spec. Its narrow
      subject failed to capture the intended reusable planet language; its
      stamps do not apply to the revised question. The existing Moon’s radius
      and orbit are one use case, not constraints on all examples.
    </p>
    <h2 id="references">1990s games that depicted planets</h2>
    <p>
      This survey identifies games with visible planets, rather than only named
      worlds in their fiction. The descriptions distinguish combat bodies,
      system views, planet portraits and landable worlds. They establish a
      reference pool. Selected images and visual commentary are now in
      <a href="/lab/studies/mercury-2026-11-12#02-1"
        >02.1 / Game-art references</a
      >;
      <a href="/lab/studies/mercury-2026-11-12#02-2"
        >02.2 / Astronomical references</a
      >
      adds NASA images with their colour methods identified.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Game / release</th>
          <th>Planet depiction</th>
          <th>Source</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Starflight / Genesis version, 1991</td>
          <td>
            Orbital exploration and surface landing; a 1990s version of a 1986
            game.
          </td>
          <td>
            <a
              href="https://gamefaqs.gamespot.com/genesis/586489-starflight/faqs/7072"
              >Version-specific guide</a
            >
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Star Control II, 1992</td>
          <td>
            Super Melee combat planets, system bodies and planetary inspection.
          </td>
          <td>
            <a href="https://wiki.uqm.stack.nl/Star_Control_II"
              >Game reference</a
            >
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Master of Orion, 1993</td>
          <td>
            Planet portraits and distinct environments including barren, dead
            and inferno.
          </td>
          <td>
            <a
              href="https://drwjf.github.io/ad3/references/Master_of_Orion_1993_Manual_EN.pdf"
              >Original manual</a
            >
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Frontier: Elite II, 1993</td>
          <td>Solid 3D planets approached from space and landed on.</td>
          <td>
            <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frontier%3A_Elite_II"
              >Game overview</a
            >
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Ascendancy, 1995</td>
          <td>
            Planet sprites in navigable system views and separate surface
            management.
          </td>
          <td>
            <a href="https://forceforgood.co.uk/strategy/ascendancy/"
              >Illustrated review</a
            >
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Master of Orion II, 1996</td>
          <td>
            Multiple planets per system, planet pictures and colony views;
            barren rock is an explicit environment.
          </td>
          <td>
            <a
              href="https://device.report/m/10c6fa2556c2241e320074ec5681b9755caccc87749e24e5a1e301d1be60c9f0.pdf"
              >Original manual</a
            >
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Escape Velocity, 1996</td>
          <td>Planet bodies in top-down flight and landing destinations.</td>
          <td>
            <a href="https://www.macintoshrepository.org/6700-escape-velocity"
              >Game and screenshots</a
            >
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Imperium Galactica, 1997</td>
          <td>Planetary colonies and surface construction views.</td>
          <td>
            <a href="https://www.mobygames.com/game/280/imperium-galactica/"
              >Game overview</a
            >
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Star Trek: Birth of the Federation, 1999</td>
          <td>
            Small system-view planets with classes and terraforming states.
          </td>
          <td>
            <a
              href="https://gaming.trekcore.com/birthofthefederation/downloads/tutorial.pdf"
              >Original tutorial</a
            >
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      For this study, the strongest initial comparisons are Star Control II’s
      combat planet and Escape Velocity’s flight bodies for gameplay-scale
      reading, plus Master of Orion I/II for environmental identity. This is a
      relevance judgment, not a selected art direction. Frontier supplies a
      contrasting solid-3D approach; colony surface views are less direct
      references for a whole planet seen during flight.
    </p>
    <h3>Supporting reference notes</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>
        <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mercury/facts/"
          >NASA: Mercury facts</a
        >
      </dt>
      <dd>
        Physical anchor: grey-brown rock, impact craters, extreme day/night
        temperatures and a thin exosphere. Inference for exploration: test harsh
        directional light and restrained relief before using lava as shorthand
        for proximity. The charging field is a game mechanic, not a scientific
        boundary around the real Sun.
      </dd>
      <dt>
        <a
          href="https://science.nasa.gov/photojournal/mercurys-true-color-is-in-the-eye-of-the-beholder/"
          >NASA: Mercury’s “True” Color</a
        >
      </dt>
      <dd>
        Distinguishes subtle visible colour from enhanced-colour imagery. Useful
        for separating surface material from an artistic heat palette; it does
        not prescribe the game’s palette.
      </dd>
      <dt>
        <a href="https://sc2.sourceforge.net/screenshots.php"
          >Star Control II / The Ur-Quan Masters: project screenshots</a
        >
      </dt>
      <dd>
        <a href="https://wiki.uqm.stack.nl/Star_Control_II"
          >Star Control II (1992)</a
        >
        supplies both the Super Melee combat planet and larger planetary
        inspection views. The combat planet is the direct reference for a body
        seen during flight; inspection views offer a separate surface-detail
        comparison. The Ur-Quan Masters is a later port, so its screenshot
        versions must be distinguished from original DOS/3DO captures. Images
        are considered in section 02.1 through the Mercury surface capture.
      </dd>
      <dt>
        <a
          href="https://drwjf.github.io/ad3/references/Master_of_Orion_1993_Manual_EN.pdf"
          >Master of Orion (1993): original manual</a
        >
      </dt>
      <dd>
        Printed page 12 separates barren, dead and inferno environments;
        barrenness and extreme heat are not interchangeable categories.
        Comparison target: how planet portraits communicate material and
        environment with limited colour. Manual text was inspected; portrait is
        compared in 02.1 through a Terran portrait sourced from OldGames.sk.
      </dd>
      <dt>
        <a
          href="https://gaming.trekcore.com/birthofthefederation/downloads/tutorial.pdf"
          >Star Trek: Birth of the Federation (1999): tutorial</a
        >
      </dt>
      <dd>
        Printed pages 5–6 describe the small planet icons and their class
        labels. Comparison target: what the body communicates unaided and what
        relies on adjacent text. Tutorial text was inspected; screenshot
        rendering was unavailable in the research tool, so no visual conclusion
        is recorded. This is one concrete Star Trek reference, not an assumed
        identification of every title mentioned in the brief.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      These sources start the comparison set. Neither their art nor NASA
      photographs become shipped assets. External reference research does not
      relax the current procedural-art direction.
    </p>
    <h2>Current implementation evidence</h2>
    <p>
      packages/core/src/lib/assets/planet.ts draws the disc, outline and
      optional ring. packages/core/src/lib/config.ts supplies a radius-18 rock
      named “The Moon” at orbit radius 300, the innermost of three configured
      bodies; there is no Mercury identity yet. initializePlanets in
      packages/core/src/lib/physics/planets.ts supplies the roster shared by
      modes. packages/mode-a/src/lib/simulation.ts fixes the practice phase and
      assigns “G7, Sun”. Mercury is intended for practice; the shared roster
      must not accidentally rename the body in every mode. The visual language,
      however, is intended for rock planets in charging fields across modes and
      sizes; the current roster does not bound the design.
    </p>
    <p>
      docs/design.md owns procedural art and readability. The planet feature
      spec (docs/specs/game/world/planets/spec.md) describes the existing flat
      style but includes historical random-spawn claims that differ from the
      deterministic production initializer. It supplies no authority for a new
      surface design. The virtual-home-system lore and Mercury identity are
      supplied intent for this cycle, not claims that those features already
      exist in play.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>

